\DOC add_simp

\TYPE {add_simp : thm -> unit}

\SYNOPSIS
Declare a theorem into the simp set.

\DESCRIBE
A call {add_simp th} adds {th} to the set of simplification rules that
{SIMPS_CONV}, {SIMPS_RULE}, {SIMPS_TAC} and {ASM_SIMPS_TAC} use, as
Isabelle's {[simp]} and Lean's {@[simp]} do. The theorem is processed
into rewrites the way {SIMP_CONV} would process it, so a conditional
equation, a conjunction of equations and a plain fact are all allowed.

The set is global, as {basic_rewrites} is, and it is a set you declare
into as you go rather than one you pass about. {extend_basic_rewrites}
is the nearest thing HOL Light has, but that is the set {REWRITE_TAC}
silently uses everywhere, so adding a rule to it changes every proof in
the session; this set is only used where it is asked for.

The cost of adding is one insertion into the term net, and the cost of
using the set does not depend on how big it is -- which is why a
development may declare thousands of rules and still simplify quickly.
{SIMP_TAC thl} builds a net out of {thl} on every call, so the same rules
passed as a list would be rebuilt every time.

\FAILURE
Never fails.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # add_simps (CONJUNCTS count);;
  val it : unit = ()

  # SIMPS_CONV [ADD_CLAUSES] `count (Call (Cons (Num 3) Nil))`;;
  val it : thm = |- count (Call (Cons (Num 3) Nil)) = 1
}

\COMMENTS
A rule that loops will loop every proof that simplifies afterwards;
{del_simp} takes one out again.

\SEEALSO
add_simps, add_type_simps, ASM_SIMPS_TAC, del_simp,
extend_basic_rewrites, simps, SIMPS_TAC.

\ENDDOC
